-- Topic: Incident Response and Recovery
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:6 / L2:10 / L3:9 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L3 — BIA must precede DR plan development
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Cascade Manufacturing''s new CTO wants to develop a comprehensive disaster recovery plan in January 2026. The IT director asks whether the team should begin by selecting a DR site or by first completing a business impact analysis. Under NIST SP 800-34 guidelines, what should the team do first?', '["Conduct a business impact analysis to identify critical functions and establish recovery priorities", "Select and provision a hot site to ensure the fastest possible recovery time", "Draft the disaster recovery plan and then validate its assumptions through testing", "Implement backup systems for all servers before analyzing business requirements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, the BIA must be completed before designing the DR plan because it identifies critical business functions, their dependencies, and acceptable downtime thresholds (RTO, RPO, MTD). The BIA provides the recovery requirements that drive all subsequent DR decisions. Selecting a DR site first (choice B) skips the requirements phase and risks over- or under-provisioning recovery resources for the organization''s actual needs.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Write blockers in digital forensics
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'What is the purpose of a write blocker in digital forensics?', '["It encrypts evidence files to prevent unauthorized viewing", "It creates a backup copy of the original storage media", "It prevents any data from being written to the original evidence media", "It blocks network traffic to isolate a compromised system"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-86 (Guide to Integrating Forensic Techniques), a write blocker is a hardware or software tool that prevents any writes to the original evidence storage media during examination. This preserves the integrity of the original evidence for legal admissibility. Creating a backup copy (choice B) describes forensic imaging, which is a separate procedure that may use write blockers but is not the same function.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — GDPR 72-hour notification deadline
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'On February 10, 2026, Northgate Analytics, a London-based fintech company, discovers that a database containing 12,000 EU customer records was exposed to unauthorized access for six days. Under the General Data Protection Regulation, how quickly must Northgate notify the supervisory authority?', '["24 hours from discovery of the breach", "72 hours from becoming aware of the breach", "60 days from discovery of the breach", "30 days from the date the breach began"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GDPR Article 33, a data controller must notify the relevant supervisory authority within 72 hours of becoming aware of a personal data breach, unless the breach is unlikely to result in a risk to individuals'' rights. The 60-day timeline (choice C) is the HIPAA notification requirement for covered entities, not GDPR. GDPR''s 72-hour window is significantly shorter and reflects the EU''s emphasis on prompt transparency.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Warm site characteristics
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Bridgeport Insurance is evaluating disaster recovery site options in Q1 2026. The DR architect describes a facility that has network infrastructure, basic hardware, and power systems installed but requires the latest data to be loaded and applications configured before processing transactions. Which DR site type does this describe?', '["Hot site", "Cold site", "Mobile site", "Warm site"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A warm site is a partially equipped facility with basic infrastructure (network, hardware, power) installed, but it requires additional configuration and data restoration before becoming operational, typically taking hours to activate. A hot site (choice A) is fully operational with real-time data replication and can provide near-instant failover. A cold site (choice B) has only basic utilities with no IT equipment installed.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Parallel DR test
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Atlas Regional Bank conducts an annual DR test in February 2026 by activating its backup data center while the primary data center continues to process all customer transactions. The DR team verifies that the backup systems can handle the workload without actually routing live traffic. Which type of DR test is Atlas performing?', '["Parallel test", "Full interruption test", "Tabletop walkthrough", "Simulation test"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, a parallel test activates backup systems alongside production to verify their readiness without disrupting normal operations. Live traffic continues on the primary systems. A full interruption test (choice B) shuts down production systems entirely and routes operations to the backup site, which carries significant risk if the failover fails. Atlas avoided that risk by keeping its primary data center active.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Business impact analysis definition
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'What is the primary purpose of a business impact analysis in disaster recovery planning?', '["To test whether backup systems can handle production workloads", "To select the appropriate type of disaster recovery site", "To identify critical business functions and determine acceptable downtime thresholds", "To document the chain of custody for digital evidence"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, a BIA identifies all critical business processes, assesses the impact of their disruption over time, and establishes recovery requirements including RTO, RPO, and MTD. These requirements drive the design of the DR plan. Testing backup systems (choice A) describes DR testing, which occurs after the plan is built. The BIA is a prerequisite that defines what the DR plan must achieve.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: easy / L2 — Attorney-client privilege in IR
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'After confirming a data breach on January 15, 2026, Whitfield Capital''s CISO immediately contacts the company''s general counsel before sharing details with any other department. The primary reason for engaging legal counsel first is to:', '["Ensure the breach is reported to regulators within 24 hours", "Establish attorney-client privilege over incident response communications", "Delegate the CISO''s incident response duties to the legal team", "Prevent the IR team from collecting digital evidence"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, engaging legal counsel early in incident response helps establish attorney-client privilege, protecting sensitive communications and work product from disclosure in potential litigation. This does not mean legal takes over IR duties (choice C) — the CISO retains operational control. Early legal engagement ensures investigation findings and internal discussions receive legal protection while response activities proceed.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — BIA-driven RTO vs MTD gap requiring investment
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Pinnacle Healthcare operates three critical systems: the EHR (MTD 8 hours, current RTO 6 hours), the pharmacy dispensing system (MTD 4 hours, current RTO 12 hours), and the billing platform (MTD 48 hours, current RTO 24 hours). The CTO has budget to upgrade only one system''s DR capabilities. Based on BIA principles, which system should receive the investment?', '["The EHR, because electronic health records affect patient safety", "The billing platform, because it generates revenue", "All three systems equally, because every critical system needs the same RTO", "The pharmacy dispensing system, because its RTO exceeds its MTD"]'::jsonb, 3, 'BIA-driven DR planning per NIST SP 800-34 requires that each system''s RTO remain within its MTD. The pharmacy system''s 12-hour RTO exceeds its 4-hour MTD, meaning the organization cannot recover before irreversible harm occurs. The EHR (choice A) already meets its MTD with a 6-hour RTO within an 8-hour window. The pharmacy system is the only one where current capabilities fail to meet business requirements.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Cyber insurance notification timing
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Granite Technology suffers a ransomware attack on February 3, 2026. The CFO asks the IR team when to notify the company''s cyber insurance carrier. Under typical cyber insurance policy terms, when should the notification occur?', '["As soon as reasonably practicable after discovering the incident", "Only after the incident has been fully resolved and remediated", "Within 72 hours, as required by the GDPR", "Only if the ransom demand exceeds the policy deductible"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, cyber insurance policies typically require prompt notification to preserve coverage — ideally as soon as reasonably practicable after discovering a qualifying incident. Waiting until full resolution (choice B) risks violating policy terms and may void coverage, especially if the insurer''s preferred vendors were not engaged during the response. GDPR''s 72-hour requirement (choice C) applies to supervisory authority notification, not insurance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Full interruption DR test carries highest risk
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Evergreen Mutual Bank''s board of directors mandates a DR test in March 2026 that shuts down the primary data center entirely and routes all customer-facing operations to the backup facility for 48 hours. The CTO warns that this approach carries the highest risk of any DR testing method. Which test type is the board requiring?', '["Parallel test", "Tabletop walkthrough", "Full interruption test", "Simulation test"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, a full interruption test shuts down production systems and transfers all operations to the DR site, providing the most realistic validation of recovery capabilities. The CTO''s warning is correct — this carries the highest disruption level because a failure at the backup site could leave the bank without operational systems. A parallel test (choice A) avoids this risk by keeping the primary site running simultaneously.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — HIPAA breach notification for 500+ individuals requires media notice
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'On January 22, 2026, Beacon Health System discovers a breach affecting 2,400 patient records containing protected health information. Under HIPAA breach notification requirements, which notification obligation applies because the breach affects more than 500 individuals?', '["Notify the state attorney general within 24 hours", "Notify HHS, affected individuals, and prominent media outlets serving the affected area", "Notify only the affected individuals by certified mail within 30 days", "Notify the FBI Cyber Division and suspend all affected systems"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under HIPAA''s Breach Notification Rule (45 CFR §§ 164.404-164.408), when a breach affects 500 or more individuals, the covered entity must notify HHS, each affected individual, and prominent media outlets serving the state or jurisdiction, all within 60 days. Notifying only individuals (choice C) omits the mandatory HHS and media notifications required for breaches of this scale.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Short-term vs long-term containment distinction
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'During an active intrusion at Redfield Manufacturing on February 5, 2026, the IR team first disconnects the compromised server from the network to stop lateral movement. Over the next 12 hours, the team applies a temporary firewall rule to block the attacker''s IP range while preserving system logs for forensic analysis. The initial disconnection represents short-term containment. What does the 12-hour follow-up represent?', '["The eradication phase", "The recovery phase", "The identification phase", "Long-term containment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, containment has two sub-phases: short-term containment (immediate isolation to stop the attack) and long-term containment (temporary fixes that allow operations to resume while preserving evidence). Applying firewall rules while maintaining forensic evidence characterizes long-term containment. Eradication (choice A) involves removing the root cause entirely, such as deleting malware and patching vulnerabilities, which has not yet occurred.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Why containment must precede eradication
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Apex Dynamics detects active ransomware on February 8, 2026. The IT manager argues the team should skip containment and immediately begin eradication by wiping and rebuilding all affected servers to save time. The CISO overrules this decision. Which combination of risks justifies requiring containment before eradication?', '["Eradication is more expensive than containment, and the budget is limited", "Regulatory agencies require documented containment before accepting an incident report", "Containment provides time to negotiate with the ransomware operator", "Skipping containment risks destroying forensic evidence and allows the threat to spread to unaffected systems during eradication"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, containment must precede eradication because it isolates affected systems, preventing the threat from spreading, and preserves volatile evidence needed for forensic analysis and legal proceedings. Wiping servers immediately destroys evidence and may miss compromised systems not yet identified. The cost argument (choice A) is secondary — the primary concern is evidence preservation and threat isolation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Failover definition
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'What is failover in the context of disaster recovery?', '["Switching operations to a backup system when the primary system fails", "Conducting a post-incident review after a disaster", "Restoring data from magnetic tape archives", "Testing the DR plan by shutting down production systems"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, failover is the process of automatically or manually switching operations from a failed primary system to a backup system or site to ensure continuity of operations during a disruption. Testing by shutting down production (choice D) describes a full interruption DR test, which is a planned exercise rather than an operational switch triggered by an actual system failure.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — SIEM role in identification phase
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Crestline Pharmaceuticals'' security operations center uses a SIEM platform that correlates firewall logs, endpoint alerts, and authentication records. On February 12, 2026, the SIEM generates a high-priority alert after correlating a failed VPN login from an unusual country, a successful login two minutes later, and an immediate 500 MB download from a restricted file share. Under NIST SP 800-61, which IR phase is the SIEM supporting?', '["Preparation", "Containment", "Identification", "Recovery"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, the identification phase involves detecting and confirming that a security incident has occurred through monitoring alerts, analyzing logs, and correlating events. The SIEM''s cross-source correlation of suspicious login patterns and data exfiltration indicators is a core identification activity. Preparation (choice A) involves building readiness before incidents, such as deploying the SIEM itself — but using it to detect threats falls under identification.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — IR playbook selection for specific incident type
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'On February 15, 2026, Sterling Financial''s SOC confirms that an employee''s email account was compromised through a phishing link, and the attacker is using the account to send fraudulent wire transfer requests to vendors. The IR team opens the incident response plan and selects the appropriate playbook. Which playbook should the team follow?', '["The ransomware response playbook", "The business email compromise playbook", "The distributed denial-of-service playbook", "The insider threat playbook"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, organizations should maintain playbooks for specific incident types to ensure a structured response. This scenario describes a business email compromise (BEC) — a compromised email account used for fraudulent financial requests. The insider threat playbook (choice D) addresses malicious or negligent insiders acting on their own behalf, not external attackers impersonating employees through compromised accounts.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L2 — Cold site characteristics
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Oakmont Logistics is evaluating DR sites in Q1 2026. One option is a leased warehouse with electrical power and HVAC but no IT equipment, servers, or network infrastructure installed. The vendor estimates it would take three to five days to become operational. Which DR site type does this describe?', '["Hot site", "Warm site", "Mirror site", "Cold site"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A cold site is a facility with only basic environmental infrastructure (power, HVAC, physical security) but no pre-installed IT equipment. It requires hardware procurement, installation, and data restoration before becoming operational, typically taking days. A warm site (choice B) would already have network infrastructure and basic hardware installed, reducing activation time to hours rather than the days required for a cold site.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — SEC Form 8-K filing requirement for material cyber incidents
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'On February 18, 2026, a publicly traded retailer determines that a cybersecurity incident involving the theft of 3 million credit card numbers is material to investors. Under SEC cybersecurity disclosure rules, within what timeframe must the company file a Form 8-K?', '["72 hours from discovery", "60 calendar days from discovery", "4 business days from determining materiality", "30 calendar days from the date of the breach"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under SEC rules, public companies must disclose material cybersecurity incidents on Form 8-K within 4 business days after determining the incident is material. The 72-hour timeline (choice A) is the GDPR supervisory authority notification requirement, not the SEC filing deadline. The trigger for the SEC timeline is the materiality determination, not the initial discovery of the breach.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — IR team cross-functional composition
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Meridian Capital''s CISO is assembling a cross-functional incident response team in Q1 2026. The team must include members from multiple departments to ensure comprehensive incident handling. According to NIST SP 800-61, which set of departments should be represented on the IR team?', '["Only the IT security department and the help desk", "IT security, facilities management, and the mail room", "IT security and the external forensics vendor exclusively", "IT security, legal, communications, HR, and executive management"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, an effective IR team should be cross-functional, including IT security for technical response, legal counsel for regulatory and litigation guidance, communications for stakeholder messaging, HR for insider-related incidents, and executive management for strategic decisions. Limiting the team to IT security and help desk (choice A) omits legal, communications, and executive perspectives essential for comprehensive incident handling.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — BIA criticality ranking drives proportional resource allocation
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Harborside Financial completes a BIA in January 2026 identifying four critical systems: wire transfers ($180,000/hour downtime loss), customer portal ($12,000/hour), internal email ($3,000/hour), and HR payroll ($800/hour). The CTO must allocate a limited DR budget. Which BIA-driven principle should guide the allocation?', '["Allocate equal DR budgets to all four systems because all are identified as critical", "Allocate DR resources proportionally to each system''s financial impact per hour of downtime", "Allocate the entire budget to the wire transfer system and accept risk on the others", "Defer the allocation decision until after a disaster occurs and actual losses are known"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, the BIA''s impact analysis and criticality rankings should drive proportional resource allocation. Systems with higher financial impact per hour of downtime warrant stronger DR investments such as lower RTOs and more resilient backups. Equal allocation (choice A) ignores vastly different impact levels — wire transfers at $180K/hour clearly require more robust recovery than HR payroll at $800/hour.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Simulation DR test characteristics
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Cornerstone Credit Union conducts a DR exercise in February 2026 where the IR team responds to a realistic hurricane scenario by executing communication procedures, activating backup systems in a test environment, and coordinating with third-party vendors — all without affecting member-facing production systems. Which type of DR test is this?', '["Full interruption test", "Tabletop walkthrough", "Parallel test", "Simulation test"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, a simulation test involves the team responding to a realistic scenario by performing actual response actions (communications, system activations) without affecting production systems, causing minimal disruption. A tabletop walkthrough (choice B) involves discussion only — participants talk through procedures without taking any actions. The simulation goes further by executing real procedures in a test environment while keeping production untouched.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Maximum Tolerable Downtime definition
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'What does Maximum Tolerable Downtime represent in disaster recovery planning?', '["The longest period a business function can be unavailable before causing irreversible harm", "The maximum amount of data that can be lost during a disruption", "The time required to restore systems from the most recent backup", "The total cost of a system outage measured over one fiscal year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, Maximum Tolerable Downtime (MTD) represents the absolute maximum time a business function can be disrupted before the organization suffers irreversible damage — financial collapse, regulatory penalties, or permanent reputational harm. The maximum acceptable data loss (choice B) describes the Recovery Point Objective (RPO), which is measured in time but refers to data currency, not system availability duration.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Containment must precede eradication in scenario
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'On February 20, 2026, Ironclad Defense Corp.''s IR team identifies malware on five workstations in the engineering department. A junior analyst wants to immediately delete the malware and rebuild the systems. The IR team lead insists on isolating the workstations from the network first. Why is the team lead''s approach correct under NIST SP 800-61?', '["Isolation is required before the IR team can request a forensic imaging warrant", "Malware removal tools cannot function unless the system is disconnected from the network", "Containment prevents further spread and preserves evidence before the root cause is removed", "Eradication can only be performed by external forensic consultants after containment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, containment must precede eradication. Isolating affected systems prevents malware from spreading to additional workstations and preserves volatile evidence (RAM, network connections) needed for forensic analysis. Jumping directly to eradication risks destroying evidence and allows undetected infections on other systems to continue operating. Containment establishes the safe boundary for all subsequent response activities.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Volatile data must be captured before system shutdown
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Riverton Aerospace''s IR team arrives at a compromised server still running on February 22, 2026. The system administrator asks whether to shut down the server immediately to prevent further damage. The forensic examiner objects, insisting on performing specific actions before any shutdown. According to digital forensics best practices, what must be captured before powering off?', '["Hard drive contents and external backup tapes", "RAM contents, active network connections, and running processes", "The server''s BIOS configuration and firmware version", "The physical location and serial number of the server hardware"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-86, evidence should be collected in order of volatility — most volatile first. RAM contents, active network connections, and running processes are highly volatile and lost immediately upon shutdown. Hard drive contents (choice A) are non-volatile and persist after power-off, making them lower priority for immediate capture. Failing to capture volatile data before shutdown permanently destroys potential evidence of attacker activity.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Security incident definition
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'What defines a security incident in the context of incident response?', '["Any routine software update that requires system downtime", "A scheduled penetration test performed by an authorized vendor", "A natural disaster that damages only the physical facility", "Any event that compromises the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of an information asset"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, a security incident is any event that actually or potentially compromises the confidentiality, integrity, or availability (CIA) of information assets. This encompasses unauthorized access, data breaches, malware infections, and denial-of-service attacks. A scheduled penetration test (choice B) is an authorized activity and does not constitute an incident, even though it mimics attack techniques.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — BIA financial impact drives RTO prioritization
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Olympus Manufacturing completes a BIA in February 2026. The analysis shows that its order fulfillment system generates $45,000 per hour in revenue while the company website generates $2,000 per hour. The DR architect must set RTOs for both systems. Based on the BIA results, how should the RTOs compare?', '["Both systems should have identical RTOs since both are customer-facing", "The website should have the shorter RTO because it is the public face of the company", "The order fulfillment system should have the shorter RTO because its hourly financial impact is significantly higher", "Neither system needs a defined RTO because the BIA only establishes MTD values"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, the BIA''s impact analysis directly drives RTO assignment. Systems with higher financial impact per hour of downtime require shorter RTOs to minimize business losses. At $45,000/hour versus $2,000/hour, the order fulfillment system''s downtime is far more costly, justifying a more aggressive recovery target. Assigning identical RTOs (choice A) ignores the BIA''s core purpose of differentiating recovery priorities based on quantified impact.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Navigating overlapping regulatory notification timelines
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'On February 24, 2026, National Health Partners (a publicly traded HIPAA-covered entity with EU patients) discovers a breach affecting 1,200 patients including 300 EU residents. The legal team must navigate overlapping regulatory timelines. Which notification sequence correctly prioritizes the most urgent deadlines?', '["GDPR supervisory authority within 72 hours, SEC Form 8-K within 4 business days of materiality determination, then HIPAA notifications within 60 days", "HIPAA notification within 60 days, then GDPR notification within 60 days, then SEC disclosure at the next quarterly filing", "SEC Form 8-K within 72 hours, then GDPR notification within 30 days, then HIPAA notification within 90 days", "Notify all three regulators simultaneously within 30 days of discovering the breach"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When multiple regulations apply, the strictest deadline governs first. GDPR requires supervisory authority notification within 72 hours (Article 33), SEC rules require Form 8-K within 4 business days of materiality determination, and HIPAA allows up to 60 days. Choice B incorrectly applies HIPAA''s 60-day timeline to GDPR and defers the SEC filing. Organizations subject to multiple frameworks must track each deadline independently and comply with the earliest first.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Recovery phase primary goal
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'What is the primary goal of the recovery phase in the incident response lifecycle?', '["To identify which systems were affected by the incident", "To restore affected systems to normal operations and monitor for recurrence", "To remove malware and patch exploited vulnerabilities", "To document findings and update the incident response plan"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, the recovery phase focuses on restoring systems to normal operations through clean backups, staged reintroduction to the network, and monitoring for signs of recurrence. Removing malware and patching vulnerabilities (choice C) describes the eradication phase, which precedes recovery. Documenting findings and updating the IR plan (choice D) describes the lessons learned phase, which follows recovery.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Untested DR plan fails during real disaster
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Zenith Financial has maintained a documented DR plan for five years but has never conducted any form of testing. In March 2026, a power grid failure forces the company to execute the plan for the first time. The failover fails because network configurations are outdated and key staff do not know their assigned roles. Which DR planning deficiency is the root cause?', '["The organization never validated the plan through any level of DR testing", "The organization selected a cold site instead of a hot site", "The DR plan was too detailed and overwhelmed the recovery team", "The power grid failure was an unforeseeable event not covered by the DR plan"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-34, DR plans must be regularly tested through progressively rigorous methods (tabletop, simulation, parallel, full interruption) to verify assumptions, identify gaps, and train personnel. An untested plan contains unvalidated assumptions — outdated configurations and untrained staff are classic symptoms of this deficiency. The site type (choice B) would affect recovery time but would not cause role confusion or configuration errors.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: hard / L3 — Post-incident review must happen within 1-2 weeks
(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery', 'Three weeks after resolving a major supply chain attack in February 2026, Vanguard Aerospace''s CISO schedules the post-incident review. Several IR team members have been reassigned and cannot recall specific decisions made during the response. Detailed notes from the containment phase were not preserved. According to NIST SP 800-61, what scheduling error contributed to this loss of institutional knowledge?', '["The post-incident review was delayed too long and should have been conducted within one to two weeks of resolution", "The review should have been conducted during the containment phase before resolution", "Post-incident reviews are only required for incidents lasting more than 30 days", "The CISO should have conducted the review alone rather than involving the full IR team"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-61, the lessons learned meeting should be held within one to two weeks after incident resolution to ensure that details are fresh in participants'' memories and documentation is still accessible. A three-week delay combined with team reassignments degraded the quality of the review. Conducting the review during containment (choice B) is impractical because the team is still actively managing the incident and cannot objectively assess the full response.', 'hard', 3)
;

COMMIT;
